ve: \"DN200/250-G1000\". With attributes.type capturing types. I'll use \"G1000-DN200/250\" style: model_id = \"G{g}-DN{body}/{cartr}\" and include attributes.type.\n\nActually, maybe better to include the type in model_id because the certified types matter: The model is really \"FMT-Lx DN200/250 G1000\". But the \"or\" indicates the same characteristics apply to those types. To avoid combinatorial duplication (FMT-Lx vs FMT-Dc have identical data), I'll treat the group row as the model variant and note applicable types.\n\nDecision: models[] entries:\n{\n  \"model_id\": \"G1000-DN200/250\",\n  \"label\": \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 200, DN cartridge 250, G 1000\",\n  \"attributes\": {\"type\": \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", \"dn_body\": 200, \"dn_cartridge\": 250, \"g_value\": \"G1000\"}\n}\n\n42 entries. For qmax etc., model_level entries with values referencing model_id.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level (constant across all models):\n- number_of_drums: value \"8 / 0\"... structured: it's two numbers (before and after decimal separator/comma). Value as string \"8/0\"? The column is \"Number of drums Before and after comma\", value \"8 / 0\". I'll make two type_level characteristics:\n  - drums_before_decimal_point: 8\n  - drums_after_decimal_point: 0\n  Or one: number_of_drums_before_after_comma: \"8/0\". Hmm. Rule 2 wants numbers where possible. I'll emit:\n  {\"drums_before_decimal_point\": {value: 8, unit_symbol: null}}, {\"drums_after_decimal_point\": {value: 0}}. But \"comma\" is the decimal separator (European). Label: \"number_of_drums_before_comma\": 8, \"number_of_drums_after_comma\": 0. I'll use those.\n\n- control_element: {value: 0.2, unit_symbol: \"m³/h\"} — the unit column says m³/h for control element. Odd but as printed. label: control_element. Hmm, control element of a drum meter is the volume per... whatever. Extract as-is.\n\n- blade_angle: {value: 45, unit_symbol: \"degree\"} — canonical unit_symbol maybe \"°\". Printed unit \"degree\". Use \"°\"? The unit row says \"degree\". I'll use unit_symbol \"degree\" as printed... Rule 3 says match canonical Unicode form when possible — \"°\" is canonical. Hmm, safer: \"°\"? The unitsdb id likely \"u:degree\". unit_symbol \"°\" fine. I'll use \"°\". Hmm, but \"degree\" printed... I'll use \"°\" since rule says canonical Unicode.\n\nActually to be safe and traceable, I'll use \"°\". And unit_id null.\n\n- blade_quantity: {value: 24}.\n\nBearing dims: these vary by DN pair but constant within group; they're strings like \"10x26x8\" (mm). Put in matrix_tables rows, and also maybe model_level? They're group-level, not per-G-model. Putting them in model_level would repeat per model (3× each). I'll keep them in matrix_tables only. Actually schema says matrix_tables preserves multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere — bearings fit matrix_tables. Good.\n\nmatrix_tables entry:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Table 2 — Main characteristics of FMT-S, FMT-Lx and FMT-Dc\",\n  \"columns\": [\"type\", \"dn_body\", \"dn_cartridge\", \"g_value\", \"qmax\", \"qt\", \"qmin_mr_1_30_0_to_101_bar_g\", \"qmin_mr_1_50_4_to_101_bar_g\", \"drums_before_after_comma\", \"control_element\", \"blade_angle\", \"blade_quantity\", \"bearing_front_standard\", \"bearing_rear_standard\", \"bearing_front_optional\", \"bearing_rear_optional\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"mm\", \"mm\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, \"m³/h\", \"°\", null, \"mm\", \"mm\", \"mm\", \"mm\"],\n  \"rows\": [...42 rows...],\n  \"description\": \"Qmin depends on measuring regulator ratio (MR 1:30 for 0–101 bar(g), MR 1:50 for 4–101 bar(g)). Bearing dimensions are inside diameter x outside diameter x width in mm; standard vs optional bearings.\",\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nRows: values numeric where numbers; type string; g_value \"G1000\"? The G-value column values are 1000, 1600... I'll record g_value as \"G1000\" string or number 1000? Column is \"G-value\", numeric. In rows I'll keep number 1000 and in model_id \"G1000\". Hmm, consistency: row {\"type\": \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", \"dn_body\": 200, \"dn_cartridge\": 250, \"g_value\": \"G1000\", \"qmax\": 1600, ...}. I'll use \"G1000\" string for clarity. Either is fine; numbers fine too. I'll use \"G1000\".\n\nBearing strings \"10x26x8\" as strings with unit mm noted in column_units.\n\nNow characteristics.model_level — the four flow attributes × 42 values. That is huge. Let me weigh: output length limit. 42 values each ~1 line → 4 attributes × 42 lines ≈ 170 lines plus models 42×~5 lines ≈ 210 lines plus matrix rows 42×1 line (if compact) — total maybe 500-600 lines. That's a big JSON but acceptable.\n\nHmm, could I reduce by grouping models? Alternatively, use config_level for G-value axis: axis \"g_value\" with 42 conditions? Same size.\n\nAlternatively: is it acceptable to populate model_level only in matrix_tables and provide model_level with the four attributes? The schema's \"MOST IMPORTANT\" rule says varying attributes go in model_level. I'll do it fully.\n\nLet me define model_ids compactly:\n\ng1: G1000-DN200/250, G1600-DN200/250, G2500-DN200/250\ng2: G1000-DN250/250, G1600-DN250/250, G2500-DN250/250\ng3: G1600-DN250/300, G2500-DN250/300, G4000-DN250/300\ng4: G1000-DN300/250, G1600-DN300/250, G2500-DN300/250\ng5: G1600-DN300/300, G2500-DN300/300, G4000-DN300/300\ng6: G2500-DN300/400, G4000-DN300/400, G6500-DN300/400\ng7: G1600-DN400/300, G2500-DN400/300, G4000-DN400/300\ng8: G2500-DN400/400, G4000-DN400/400, G6500-DN400/400\ng9: G4000-DN400/500, G6500-DN400/500, G10000-DN400/500\ng10: G2500-DN500/400, G4000-DN500/400, G6500-DN500/400\ng11: G4000-DN500/500, G6500-DN500/500, G10000-DN500/500\ng12: G6500-DN500/600, G10000-DN500/600, G16000-DN500/600\ng13: G4000-DN600/500, G6500-DN600/500, G10000-DN600/500\ng14: G6500-DN600/600, G10000-DN600/600, G16000-DN600/600\n\nWait — g6 Qmin: as printed, row A: G2500: Qmax 4000, Qt 80, Qmin1:30 133, Qmin1:50 80. Hmm, that really looks like an OCR error in source (Qt should be 800, Qmin 125/80 pattern elsewhere). But also possible the original document has these values. Hmm, let me think about the pattern: For g6 first row: \"2500 | 4000 | 80 | 133 | 80\". Compare g10 (500/400) first row: \"2500 | 4000 | 80 | 133 | 80\" identical. And g8 (400/400) first row identical. Meanwhile other groups' G2500 rows: \"2500 | 4000 | 800 | 125 | 80\". So the 400-cartridge groups have Qt 80?? That seems physically odd — G2500 with Qt=80 m³/h while Qmax=4000? Qt normally ≈ 0.2×Qmax = 800. And Qmin(1:30)=133 > Qt=80?? Qmin should be < Qt. So 80 and 133 are likely OCR misreads of 800 and 125/133? Hmm. Actually wait, maybe I mis-assign columns. Let me recount cell by cell for g6 row A:\n\n`<td>2500</td><td>4000</td><td>80</td><td>133</td><td>80</td>`\nG=2500, Qmax=4000, Qt=80, Qmin(1:30)=133, Qmin(1:50)=80.\n\nCompare g6 row B: `4000 | 6500 | 1300 | 216 | 130` — G4000: Qt=1300. So within the same group, G2500 has Qt=80 but G4000 Qt=1300? Inconsistent — 80 likely truncated from 800. But rule: extract as printed, don't fabricate/fix. Hmm... The instruction says don't fabricate, but also \"Decimal point... convert\" — fixing OCR is not in scope. However, \"133\" vs 125: maybe original table genuinely has different values for the 400 cartridge (larger bore). Possibly Qt=800 with Qmin=133? R137 requires Qt/Qmin ratio... MR 1:30 → Qmin = Qt/30? For Qt=1300, Qmin=216 ≈ 1300/6? no. 1300/216 ≈ 6. Hmm, actually MR = measuring regulator ratio 1:30 means Qmin = Qt/30? For g1 G1000: Qt=320, Qmin=50: 320/50=6.4. Not 30. Whatever — internal ratios don't matter.\n\nGiven ambiguity, I'll extract literally: Qt=80, Qmin=133. Actually hold on — maybe the original document really does say 800 and the markdown cell got mangled with a missing zero and 125→133? \"125\" vs \"133\": both plausible OCR. I can't resolve; extract as-is. Note: I could add a footnote noting the anomaly? Rule 7 is for actual footnotes. No.\n\nOK extract literally.\n\nNow, will I include qmin attributes with the bar(g) conditions embedded in attribute label:\n- attribute: \"qmin_mr_1_30\" with a note? Better: attribute \"qmin\" with... schema model_level: {attribute, unit, values[{model, value, footnote_markers}]}. I need two attributes distinguished by pressure range/regulator ratio:\n  - \"qmin_at_0_to_101_bar_g_mr_1_30\"\n  - \"qmin_at_4_to_101_bar_g_mr_1_50\"\n\nGood.\n\nAlternatively use config_level with axis \"measuring_regulator_ratio\"/\"pressure_range\". But model_level with two attributes is clearer.\n\nNow build model_level values arrays. Let me tabulate all 42 rows in order:\n\nOrder (as in table):\n1. G1000-DN200/250: qmax 1600, qt 320, qmin30 50, qmin50 32\n2. G1600-DN200/250: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n3. G2500-DN200/250: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n4. G1000-DN250/250: 1600, 320, 50, 32\n5. G1600-DN250/250: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n6. G2500-DN250/250: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n7. G1600-DN250/300: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n8. G2500-DN250/300: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n9. G4000-DN250/300: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n10. G1000-DN300/250: 1600, 320, 50, 32\n11. G1600-DN300/250: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n12. G2500-DN300/250: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n13. G1600-DN300/300: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n14. G2500-DN300/300: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n15. G4000-DN300/300: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n16. G2500-DN300/400: 4000, 80, 133, 80\n17. G4000-DN300/400: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n18. G6500-DN300/400: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n19. G1600-DN400/300: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n20. G2500-DN400/300: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n21. G4000-DN400/300: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n22. G2500-DN400/400: 4000, 80, 133, 80\n23. G4000-DN400/400: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n24. G6500-DN400/400: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n25. G4000-DN400/500: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n26. G6500-DN400/500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n27. G10000-DN400/500: 16000, 3200, 533, 320\n28. G2500-DN500/400: 4000, 80, 133, 80\n29. G4000-DN500/400: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n30. G6500-DN500/400: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n31. G4000-DN500/500: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n32. G6500-DN500/500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n33. G10000-DN500/500: 16000, 3200, 533, 320\n34. G6500-DN500/600: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n35. G10000-DN500/600: 16000, 3200, 533, 320\n36. G16000-DN500/600: 25000, 5000, 800, 400\n37. G4000-DN600/500: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n38. G6500-DN600/500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n39. G10000-DN600/500: 16000, 3200, 533, 320\n40. G6500-DN600/600: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n41. G10000-DN600/600: 16000, 3200, 533, 320\n42. G16000-DN600/600: 25000, 5000, 800, 400\n\nModels list with attributes:\ng1 type \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", dn 200/250\ng2 type \"FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", dn 250/250\ng3 \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 250/300\ng4 \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 300/250\ng5 \"FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 300/300\ng6 \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 300/400\ng7 \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 400/300\ng8 \"FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 400/400\ng9 \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 400/500\ng10 \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 500/400\ng11 \"FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 500/500\ng12 \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 500/600\ng13 \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 600/500\ng14 \"FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 600/600\n\nGiven the enormity, maybe I should include type_level for constants and keep model_level for the 4 flow attributes. Bearings go only in matrix_tables. That's a judgment call — acceptable.\n\nAlso include qmax etc. in matrix rows.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R137\", edition... \"R137/2012\" suggests edition 2012 (OIML R137-1&2 edition 2012). The certificate number embeds 2012. Rule: canonical id \"R137\". Edition: 2012 — is that fabricating? The number R137/2012-NL1-14.03 contains 2012 which is the edition year of the recommendation. I think including edition: 2012 is reasonable. Hmm — risk of fabrication; but this is standard: NMi certs like \"R137/2012-NL1-xx.xx\" reference OIML R137 (Edition 2012). I'll include edition 2012. Actually, to be safe, I could omit edition. The fragment doesn't state the edition explicitly. Rule 8: don't fabricate. I'll omit edition to be safe. Hmm. But R137/2012 pattern strongly implies. I'll include edition: 2012 with... no, omit. Decide: omit recommendation entirely? The cert number clearly indicates R137. I'll include recommendation: {\"id\": \"R137\"} and omit edition. That's safe.\n\nAccuracy classes: not in fragment → omit.\n\ncertificate.scheme: omit (not stated).\n\ncertificate.date_issued: not in fragment → omit.\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\" (from logo \"NMi⁺\"). Include name only. Maybe \"NMi Certin\"? Not stated — just \"NMi\". I'll include issuing_authority: {\"name\": \"NMi\"}. Hmm, the logo text \"NMi⁺\" — the plus is part of logo. Name: \"NMi\".\n\ncertified_type.category: \"Gas meter\" — inferred from R137 + m³/h + bar(g). Reasonable, include.\n\ntype_designations: [\"FMT-S\", \"FMT-Lx\", \"FMT-Dc\"].\n\nNow let me also double check \"Number of drums Before and after comma: 8 / 0\" — 8 drums before comma, 0 after. So total display 8 digits, no decimals. I'll do two type_level entries.\n\nControl element value \"0,2\" → 0.2, unit m³/h. Note: control element in drum meters is often expressed as the volume per revolution — m³/h unit printed. Keep.\n\nBlade angle 45 (degree), blade qty 24.\n\nLet me now also decide whether to include dn_body/dn_cartridge in model_level (they vary per model too!). dn_body and dn_cartridge are per-model attributes. I put them in models[].attributes — good. g_value also per model. Types per model. So model_level only qmax, qt, qmin×2.\n\nNow JSON size: models 42 entries × ~6 lines = 250 lines; model_level 4 × 42 = 168 lines; matrix rows 42 lines. Plus rest. ~600 lines. OK.\n\nActually, to reduce, models entries can be single-line JSON objects. I'll write compact single-line entries. Same for model_level values — I can put several per line? JSON formatting: I'll write values arrays with one object per line for readability but they can be compact. I'll compact values arrays into fewer lines (maybe 3-4 objects per line) to save space. It just needs to be parseable.\n\nLet me draft.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R137/2012-NL1-14.03\",\n    \"project_number\": \"SO15202424\",\n    \"page_total\": 5,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Gas meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"FMT-S\", \"FMT-Lx\", \"FMT-Dc\"],\n    \"description\": \"Rotary piston gas meters, types FMT-S, FMT-Lx and FMT-Dc, in various DN body/cartridge combinations and G-sizes, pressure range 0-101 bar(g) (Qmin via measuring regulator ratio MR 1:30) or 4-101 bar(g) (MR 1:50).\"\n  },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm — description: careful not to fabricate. The bar ranges come from the table headers (0..101 bar(g), 4..101 bar(g)). MR 1:30/1:50 from header. \"Rotary piston\" — blade/drums suggests rotary displacement meter; type names FMT... I'd better keep description minimal: \"Table 2 main characteristics: types FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc; Qmin depends on measuring regulator ratio MR 1:30 (0–101 bar(g)) or MR 1:50 (4–101 bar(g)); bearing dimensions given as inside diameter × outside diameter × width in mm.\" That's derived from the fragment. OK.\n\nmodel_family: family_name \"FMT\".\n\nNow matrix_tables rows — 42 rows with all 16 columns. Column keys:\ntype, dn_body, dn_cartridge, g_value, qmax, qt, qmin_mr1_30_0_101_barg, qmin_mr1_50_4_101_barg, drums_before_after_comma, control_element, blade_angle, blade_quantity, bearing_front_standard, bearing_rear_standard, bearing_front_optional, bearing_rear_optional.\n\nUnits: dn mm, qmax m³/h, qt m³/h, qmin m³/h, control_element m³/h, blade_angle °, bearings mm.\n\nConstants repeated in every row (drums \"8/0\", control 0.2, angle 45, qty 24) — fine.\n\nBearing per group as listed.\n\nLet me write rows:\n\ng1 rows (type \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 200, 250; bearings 10x26x8 / 6x16x5 / 12x28x8 / 6x19x6):\n1. G1000: 1600, 320, 50, 32\n2. G1600: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n3. G2500: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n\ng2 (\"FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 250, 250; same bearings):\n4. G1000: 1600, 320, 50, 32\n5. G1600: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n6. G2500: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n\ng3 (\"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 250, 300; bearings 12x28x8 / 6x19x6 / 17x40x12 / 8x22x7):\n7. G1600: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n8. G2500: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n9. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n\ng4 (\"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 300, 250; bearings 10x26x8 / 6x16x5 / 12x28x8 / 6x19x6):\n10. G1000: 1600, 320, 50, 32\n11. G1600: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n12. G2500: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n\ng5 (\"FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 300, 300; bearings 12x28x8 / 6x19x6 / 17x40x12 / 8x22x7):\n13. G1600: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n14. G2500: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n15. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n\ng6 (\"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 300, 400; bearings 15x35x11 / 8x22x7 / 17x40x12 / 8x22x7):\n16. G2500: 4000, 80, 133, 80\n17. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n18. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n\ng7 (\"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 400, 300; bearings 12x28x8 / 6x19x6 / 17x40x12 / 8x22x7):\n19. G1600: 2500, 500, 80, 50\n20. G2500: 4000, 800, 125, 80\n21. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n\ng8 (\"FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 400, 400; bearings 15x35x11 / 8x22x7 / 17x40x12 / 8x22x7):\n22. G2500: 4000, 80, 133, 80\n23. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n24. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n\ng9 (\"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 400, 500; bearings 20x47x14 / 10x26x8 / 20x47x20 / 10x26x8):\n25. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n26. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n27. G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320\n\ng10 (\"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 500, 400; bearings 15x35x11 / 8x22x7 / 17x40x12 / 8x22x7):\n28. G2500: 4000, 80, 133, 80\n29. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n30. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n\ng11 (\"FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 500, 500; bearings 20x47x14 / 10x26x8 / 20x47x20 / 10x26x8):\n31. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n32. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n33. G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320\n\ng12 (\"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 500, 600; bearings 20x47x20 / 10x26x8 / 20x47x20 / 10x26x8):\n34. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n35. G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320\n36. G16000: 25000, 5000, 800, 400\n\ng13 (\"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 600, 500; bearings 20x47x14 / 10x26x8 / 20x47x20 / 10x26x8):\n37. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130\n38. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n39. G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320\n\ng14 (\"FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", 600, 600; bearings 20x47x20 / 10x26x8 / 20x47x20 / 10x26x8):\n40. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200\n41. G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320\n42. G16000: 25000, 5000, 800, 400\n\nVerify group 13 bearings: `<td rowspan=\"3\">20x47x14</td><td rowspan=\"3\">10x26x8</td><td rowspan=\"3\">20x47x20</td><td rowspan=\"3\">10x26x8</td>` ✓.\n\nVerify group 12 bearings: `20x47x20 / 10x26x8 / 20x47x20 / 10x26x8` ✓.\n\nVerify group 9: `20x47x14 / 10x26x8 / 20x47x20 / 10x26x8` ✓.\n\nNow qmin values printed as-is including the anomalous 80/133/80 for groups 6, 8, 10 row A.\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n1. qmax: values in order of models\n2. qt\n3. qmin_mr_1_30 (0..101 bar(g))\n4. qmin_mr_1_50 (4..101 bar(g))\n\nAttribute labels: \"qmax\", \"qt\", \"qmin_mr1_30_pressure_0_101_bar_g\"? Keep simpler: \"qmin_0_to_101_bar_g_mr_1_30\", \"qmin_4_to_101_bar_g_mr_1_50\". snake_case fine.\n\ntype_level:\n{\n  \"number_of_drums_before_comma\": {\"value\": 8, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"number_of_drums_after_comma\": {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"control_element\": {\"value\": 0.2, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"blade_angle\": {\"value\": 45, \"unit_symbol\": \"°\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"blade_quantity\": {\"value\": 24, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n}\n\nShould I include structure_values with unit_id always? Schema example shows unit_id optional; rule 3 says leave null unless certain. I'll include unit_id: null keys for consistency? Rule 2 shape includes unit_id. I'll include it as null everywhere.\n\nDo I add pressure range 0..101 bar(g) / 4..101 bar(g) anywhere? They're conditions on qmin. Could add config_level entries:\nconfig_level: [\n  {attribute: \"qmin\", axis: \"pressure_range_measuring_regulator\", values: [\n    {condition: \"0-101 bar(g), MR 1:30\", value: ...}, ...\n  ]}\n]\nThat duplicates model_level. Skip — 